The installer.

setup.c                A single-executable Win32 setup program, no dependencies:
                       custom-drawn pages, a licence box that reads COPYING,
                       browsable directories with live NTFS and free-space
                       validation, a disk-size chooser, the pre-flight refusals
                       from doc/installer section 4, payload and image copying,
                       driver service creation, the X server, desktop shortcuts
                       and a logon entry, image reservation with a valid-data
                       length, a restart step with an HKLM Run autostart that
                       removes itself on success, and a console conversation with
                       the guest that runs the maker script and follows its
                       progress.

Build (mingw-w64, from this directory):

    x86_64-w64-mingw32-windres setup.rc -O coff -o setup.res
    x86_64-w64-mingw32-gcc setup.c setup.res -o mocolinux-setup.exe -O2 -mwindows \
        -mcrtdll=msvcrt-os -lshlwapi -lole32 -lws2_32 -ladvapi32

setup.res carries setup.manifest, which asks for elevation. Without it NT 6
and later start this unelevated even for an administrator account -- a
double-click is not an elevated launch -- and setup gets as far as creating
the driver service before failing. The manifest declares no supportedOS
block on purpose; see the comment inside it.

-mcrtdll=msvcrt-os is not optional: without it mingw-w64 16.1 links the
Universal CRT (api-ms-win-crt-*), which XP does not have at all and a
Windows 7 without KB2999226 does not have either -- the exe then fails to
start on exactly the machines it exists for. Check with
`objdump -p mocolinux-setup.exe | grep -i "dll name"`: msvcrt.dll is right,
anything api-ms-win-crt-* is wrong.

What a release directory has to contain beside it -- the `payload` array in
setup.c is the authoritative list, and a missing entry is fatal at install time:

    colinux-daemon.exe  colinux-slirp-net-daemon.exe  linux.sys  vmlinux
    COPYING  moco-boot.vbs  moco-icons.vbs  moco-term.bat  xstart1142.bat
    stop.bat  cogpu-daemon.exe  libvirglrenderer-1.dll  libepoxy-0.dll
    root-arch.img                            (the seed that builds the system)
    vcxsrv-64.1.14.2.1.installer.exe         (optional; the X server)

1.14.2.1 is not a whim: 1.14.3.0 dropped XP support, and every PE in 1.20.x
declares subsystem 6.0 and wants the Universal CRT, which a 5.2 loader refuses.

Verified end to end on the M92p on 2026-08-04: the host half, a restart, then
the guest building a full Manjaro desktop onto a fresh 30 GB image over the
network -- 217 console polls with zero timeouts, 1.3 GB downloaded, no bugcheck.

The installed `moco-boot.vbs` requests `--cpus 2`. That is now the validated
default, not an experimental opt-in: on 2026-08-09 the M92p's Windows 10 host
booted both Linux processors, ran Firefox without the former IPI deadlock, and
measured 1.978x sysbench CPU and 1.983x OpenSSL SHA-256 scaling. The daemon's
own default remains one processor so a manual `--cpus 1` comparison is easy.

Three things it got wrong for a long time, all of them worth not repeating.
It polled the build log with a command containing "RC=" and then tested the
reply for "RC=", so a console backlog that echoed the command read as a failed
install. Its reply parser fell back to returning the whole buffer when it could
not find the echo boundary, which is what handed the command text to that test.
And Next was disabled for the whole second half of the wizard, so a completed
install could not advance and the last page had all three buttons dead at once.
